# AL Language General Guidelines

## Code Style and Structure

- Write concise, technical AL code with accurate examples
- Follow Microsoft's AL coding standards and best practices
- Use built-in functions and standard AL design patterns
- Prefer modularization over code duplication
- Use descriptive variable names (e.g., IsPostingDateValid, HasCreditLimitExceeded)

## Naming Conventions

- Use **PascalCase** for all identifiers (variables, procedures, functions)
- Prefix record variables with meaningful abbreviations:
  - CustRec for Record Customer
  - ItemRec for Record Item
  - EquipmentRec for Record "TAG Equipment"
- Use meaningful procedure names that reflect their purpose
- Boolean variables should read as questions: IsValid, HasPermission, CanProcess

## Variable Declaration

    var
        TAGSetup: Record "TAG Setup";
        EquipmentRec: Record "TAG Equipment";
        WOHeader: Record "TAG Work Order Header";
        NoSeriesMgt: Codeunit "No. Series";
        IsProcessed: Boolean;
        TotalAmount: Decimal;

## Procedure Structure

- Keep procedures short and focused on a single responsibility
- Use clear parameter names prefixed with p for parameters
- Return named values when it improves readability

    procedure ProcessEquipment(pEquipmentID: Code[20]; pProcessDate: Date): Boolean
    var
        EquipmentRec: Record "TAG Equipment";
    begin
        if not EquipmentRec.Get(pEquipmentID) then
            exit(false);
        
        // Processing logic here
        exit(true);
    end;

## Error Handling

- Use TryFunction for error handling without displaying system messages
- Use TestField for required field validation
- Use Error() for business rule violations with clear messages

    TAGSetup.Get();
    TAGSetup.TestField("Key Safe Nos.");

## Database Operations

- Minimize database calls within loops; retrieve necessary data beforehand
- Use SetLoadFields to optimize field loading
- Use SetRange, SetFilter, and SetCurrentKey for efficient filtering
- Use FindSet when iterating, FindFirst for single records
- Use IsEmpty instead of Count = 0 for existence checks

    EquipmentRec.SetLoadFields(Description, "Equipment ID");
    EquipmentRec.SetRange(Status, EquipmentRec.Status::Active);
    if EquipmentRec.FindSet() then
        repeat
            // Process each record
        until EquipmentRec.Next() = 0;

## Preprocessor Directives

Use preprocessor symbols for version compatibility:

    #if CLEAN24
        NoSeriesMgt: Codeunit "No. Series";
    #else
        NoSeriesMgt: Codeunit NoSeriesManagement;
    #endif

Available preprocessor symbols: CLEAN18, CLEAN19, CLEAN20, CLEAN22, CLEAN23, CLEAN24

## Comments

- Use comments to explain complex logic or business rules
- Document public procedures and APIs
- Keep comments up-to-date with code changes

## Security

- Set appropriate permission properties for objects
- Avoid hardcoding sensitive information
- Use secure storage methods for credentials